// The free-function `invoke` resolves its name from a **runtime string value**, not from anything
// written at the call site — so the name may be computed, read from input, or chosen by a branch,
// and it still reaches a top-level binding.
//
// That is the sharp edge for any backend that wants to decide *where* to keep a top-level binding:
// the set of names reachable through this door is not knowable from the source. A literal
// `invoke("f", …)` names `f`; `invoke(names[i], …)` names whatever the list held. Any answer keyed
// on the spelling of the name would be wrong here, so the only correct reading is that a program
// containing this door can reach ANY of its top-level bindings by name — including `unused`, which
// nothing below ever mentions again.
//
// Both backends are pinned, because this is exactly where they could silently disagree: they index
// the top level with different structures, and a name that resolves in one and not the other is a
// wrong answer rather than a slow one.
// expect: stdout "Ok(2)"
// expect: stdout "Ok(30)"
// expect: stdout "miss"
// expect: stdout "unused 99"
// expect: exit 0
mut f = fn(x: int) => x + 1;
mut g = fn(x: int) => x * 10;
mut unused = 99;

names = ["f", "g", "nope"];

// A name the compiler cannot read: an element of a list, chosen at run time.
echo invoke(names[0], [1]);
echo invoke(names[1], [3]);

// The same door, still a miss for a name nothing bound.
echo match invoke(names[2], []) {
    Ok(_) => "ok",
    Err(_) => "miss",
};

// And a binding the dispatch never actually names still has to behave — it was reachable, which is
// what matters, and it reads back as itself.
echo "unused ${unused}";
